+# Guitar Flash Cards
+
+A Visual Basic .NET Windows Forms application designed to help users learn and practice reading musical notes on both treble and bass clefs. The application presents random musical notes visually and challenges users to identify them correctly, tracking their progress and accuracy.
+
+## Supported Versions/Platforms
+
+- Framework: .NET Framework 4.0 or higher
+- Platform: Windows (x86/x64)
+- Development Environment: Visual Studio 2010 or later
+
+## Quick-Start Instructions
+
+1. Clone or download the repository
+2. Ensure image folders (NotePics/, BassNotePics/, CheatNotePics/, CheatBassNotePics/) contain the required note images
+3. Open the solution in Visual Studio
+4. Build and run the application
+5. Select difficulty level (Easy, Medium, or Advanced) and clef type (Treble or Bass)
+6. Type the note name displayed in the image and press Enter to check your answer
+7. View your accuracy percentage in real-time
+
+## Documentation
+
+- Architecture Overview - Repository structure and file organization
+- License - MIT License information
+
+## Features
+
+- Three Difficulty Levels:

Easy: Limited note positions
Medium: More note positions per note
Advanced: All positions with optional timer mode
+- Dual Clef Support: Practice with treble or bass clef
+- Score Tracking: Real-time accuracy percentage display
+- Timer Mode: Auto-advance feature for advanced difficulty
+- Visual Cheat Sheet: Reference panel for note positions
+- Settings Persistence: Automatically saves and loads user preferences
+## Style Guidance
+This project follows Visual Basic .NET conventions:
+- Classes: PascalCase for class names (e.g., EasyMode, CheckAnswer)
+- Methods: PascalCase for method names (e.g., PickRandomNote(), SetLevel())
+- Variables: camelCase for local variables (e.g., note, i)
+- Shared Members: Used for static functionality across modules
+- File Organization: One class per file with descriptive names
+- Comments: Module-level docstrings describing purpose
+- String Interpolation: Uses VB.NET string interpolation syntax with $ ""
